WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.692
Sofia Lindqvist: Alright, happy Friday everyone. Let’s get started. Quick round of updates before we dive in—Kwame, want to kick us off?

2
00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:19.614
Kwame Asante: Sure! Not much from me, just wrapping up the onboarding checklist refresh. Oh, and I finally got access to the new payroll dashboard.

3
00:00:21.093 --> 00:00:29.170
Hannah Weiss: Nice, Kwame. On my end, we had two new parental leave requests this week, both pretty straightforward. No escalations so far.

4
00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:37.960
Tomás Rivera: Legal’s been quiet, which is always good. I’m still reviewing the California sick leave updates, but nothing urgent for People Ops yet.

5
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:45.222
Grace Liu: I’m mostly prepping for the Q3 engagement survey launch. Also, I’ll be out next Thursday, just FYI.

6
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:50.093
Sofia Lindqvist: Thanks, Grace. I’ll note that. Anything else before we move on?

7
00:00:51.414 --> 00:00:52.952
Kwame Asante: Nope, all good here.

8
00:00:53.475 --> 00:01:01.937
Sofia Lindqvist: Alright, let’s jump into the main item: the US Military Leave policy changes. Everyone had a chance to review the draft, right?

9
00:01:02.384 --> 00:01:10.846
Hannah Weiss: Yep, I went through it. The extension from 25 to 31 days for paid leave makes sense, aligns with the calendar month.

10
00:01:11.629 --> 00:01:18.552
Tomás Rivera: Agreed. And the supplemental pay language is clearer now, especially for folks who get paid by the military.

11
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:29.300
Grace Liu: I like that it spells out the top-up. I did wonder—do we need to clarify what documentation we’ll need from employees to process the supplemental pay?

12
00:01:29.862 --> 00:01:38.709
Kwame Asante: Good point, Grace. Right now, we just say 'proof of military pay' in the internal process doc, but it’s not in the handbook.

13
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:45.387
Sofia Lindqvist: Should we add a line about that in the policy? Or just reference the process doc?

14
00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:54.095
Tomás Rivera: I’d suggest referencing the process doc, so we don’t have to update the handbook every time the requirements change.

15
00:01:54.871 --> 00:02:02.179
Hannah Weiss: Yeah, that’s what we do for other leaves. Maybe just a sentence like, 'See internal process for required documentation.'

16
00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:09.268
Grace Liu: Works for me. As long as it’s clear for managers and employees where to look.

17
00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:14.799
Sofia Lindqvist: Alright, let’s add that reference under the Compensation section. Any other concerns?

18
00:02:15.064 --> 00:02:21.218
Kwame Asante: Just one—do we need to update the pay calculation table too? It still says 25 days.

19
00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:27.717
Sofia Lindqvist: Yes, good catch. The new table will just point to the policy, right?

20
00:02:29.004 --> 00:02:33.235
Hannah Weiss: Right, it’ll say 'see Compensation heading' instead of listing the days.

21
00:02:34.695 --> 00:02:37.387
Tomás Rivera: That’s cleaner, less risk of conflicting info.

22
00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:41.868
Grace Liu: Agreed. I think that covers it for me.

23
00:02:43.171 --> 00:02:59.710
Sofia Lindqvist: Okay, so to recap: we’re approving the new US Military Leave policy text, including the 31-day paid period and supplemental pay, with a reference to the internal process for documentation. The pay calculation table will point to the policy instead of listing specifics.

24
00:03:00.126 --> 00:03:10.511
Kwame Asante: I’ll take the action to update both handbook pages and add the process doc reference. Should be able to get that done in the next two weeks.

25
00:03:11.342 --> 00:03:15.573
Sofia Lindqvist: Perfect, thanks Kwame. I’ll review once you’ve got the draft up.

26
00:03:16.051 --> 00:03:21.435
Hannah Weiss: Thanks, Kwame. Let me know if you need help with the Absence Management section.

27
00:03:22.157 --> 00:03:23.695
Kwame Asante: Will do, thanks Hannah.

28
00:03:23.971 --> 00:03:28.971
Sofia Lindqvist: Alright, last agenda item—survey results. Grace, you wanted to share a quick update?

29
00:03:29.664 --> 00:03:39.664
Grace Liu: Yeah, just a heads up: response rates for the last pulse survey dropped to 62%. I’ll send a reminder to managers to encourage participation next round.

30
00:03:41.145 --> 00:03:44.607
Tomás Rivera: Do we know why it dipped? Summer holidays maybe?

31
00:03:45.151 --> 00:03:50.151
Grace Liu: That’s my guess. I’ll add a note in the comms to acknowledge that.

32
00:03:51.371 --> 00:03:55.601
Sofia Lindqvist: Sounds good. Let’s aim to get back above 70% next time.

33
00:03:56.393 --> 00:04:00.239
Kwame Asante: I can help draft the reminder if you want, Grace.

34
00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:03.681
Grace Liu: Thanks, Kwame, I’ll ping you next week.

35
00:04:05.126 --> 00:04:08.587
Sofia Lindqvist: Alright, I think that’s everything. Thanks everyone—enjoy your weekend!

36
00:04:10.081 --> 00:04:12.389
Hannah Weiss: Thanks all, have a good one!

37
00:04:13.311 --> 00:04:14.811
Tomás Rivera: Take care, everyone.

38
00:04:15.945 --> 00:04:17.445
Grace Liu: Bye!

39
00:04:17.847 --> 00:04:19.385
Kwame Asante: See you next week!
